<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Peters Humphrey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1905–1995</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Peters Humphrey was a Canadian legal scholar at McGill University who served as the first director of the Human Rights Division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Secretariat from 1946 to 1966. He prepared the first draft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a>, adopted in 1948, and later helped found Amnesty International <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and other human rights bodies.</p></div>
